X-Open-WebUI-Sdist-SHA256: e28c4fa997bf0a678caa7a0db6441da2e0c33b9a4120677f959ec3e45fccf9e9
X-Open-WebUI-Init-SHA256: 11cd2fad929db12c687795239ab3b6af1b5ea6f3ad7363deedfabf9651dd22d4
X-Open-WebUI-Auth-SHA256: d78b5f3fc2249d0b1719d0d280eb9aeef83922ef7b55c87cd843eedbd2e25c34
X-Open-WebUI-Auth-Router-SHA256: 32bf3812b9a44face4bfa7efe4a0770577dc8608127bcbb41f25ea15a6085945

Require the root-controlled current epoch before the service imports the app or
binds its listener. Stamp every Open WebUI session JWT at the single minting
seam and reject any token whose claim is missing, differently typed, lower, or
higher at the single decoding seam. API-key authentication stays disabled so it
cannot bypass epoch validation.

Header and cookie authentication call decode_token directly. Socket.IO and the
terminal WebSocket both call get_verified_user_by_token, which also converges on
decode_token.

The application gets read-only credential and comparison logic only. The
forward-only ledger writer remains outside the restored runtime.

The exact bound sign-out router clears cookies unconditionally. Its actor lookup
and revocation calls reuse decode_token, so a stale epoch remains unattributed
and causes no revocation write.
